        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_452"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_453"/>
        <dc:subject>spatiotopic updating hypothesis</dc:subject>
        <dc:title>The Updating of the Representation of Visual Space in Parietal Cortex by Intended Eye Movements</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Every eye movement produces a shift in the visual image on the retina. The receptive field, or retinal response area, of an individual visual neuron moves with the eyes so that after an eye movement it covers a new portion of visual space. For some parietal neurons, the location of the receptive field is shown to shift transiently before an eye movement. In addition, nearly all parietal neurons respond when an eye movement brings the site of a previously flashed stimulus into the receptive field. Parietal cortex both anticipates the retinal consequences of eye movements and updates the retinal coordinates of remembered stimuli to generate a continuously accurate representation of visual space.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>1992-01-03</dc:date>
        <z:citationKey>duhamelUpdatingRepresentationVisual1992</z:citationKey>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.1553535</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-05-26 11:42:26</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <z:libraryCatalog>science.org (Atypon)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <bib:pages>90-92</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
